What the endpoint needs What to use Business data with the standard envelope <code>humatransport.Register</code> HTTP 201 after creating a resource <code>Register</code> with <code>DefaultStatus: http.StatusCreated</code> A public business failure Return <code>response.NewError(status, message)</code> Response headers or a custom body <code>humatransport.RegisterOutput</code> A stream or HTTP 204 with no body <code>RegisterOutput</code> with Huma's corresponding output type <p>A nil result from <code>Register</code> is returned as <code>data: null</code> within the envelope. Creation responses use business code <code>201</code> and message <code>Created</code>. Other successful <code>Register</code> responses use code <code>0</code> and message <code>Success</code>.</p> <p>Business error codes default to the HTTP status. Set the returned error's <code>Code</code> and <code>Data</code> fields if clients need an application-specific code or public details. Huma errors use the same envelope and retain their HTTP status and explicit message. Client error details appear in <code>data.errors</code>; without details, <code>data</code> is null. For 5xx errors, causes are logged instead of included in the response. Unexpected handler errors return a generic HTTP 500 response.</p> <p>Declare error statuses in the operation's <code>Errors</code> field. Use <code>Responses</code> for custom response documentation; an entry in <code>Errors</code> overrides <code>Responses</code> for the same status.</p> <p>To combine a header with a Zodiac envelope using <code>RegisterOutput</code>, declare:</p> <pre><code>type TicketOutput struct {
    ETag string `header:"ETag"`
    Body humatransport.Body[Ticket]
}
</code></pre> <p>Return <code>&amp;TicketOutput{ETag: etag, Body: humatransport.Body[Ticket](response.OK(ticket))}</code> from its handler, where <code>ticket</code> is a <code>Ticket</code> value. See Huma response outputs for streaming and empty responses, and the registration API reference for complete signatures.</p>
